HEADIF:At the start;all
TEXT

At the start the macros used to define the macros for @code{gpm} are defined.
This is all straightforward linking of macro names to builtin functions.

END
CODE
0_define:0_pattern:;;pattern;nr01;0
0_define:0_set_var:;;set_var;nr01;0
0_define:0_get_var:;;get_var;nr01;0
0_define:0_chars_pattern:;;charpat;nr01;0
0_define:0_chars_args:;;chararg;nr01;0
0_define:0_specialchar:;;specialc;nr01;0
0_define:0_def_mcall:;;defmcall;nr01;0
0_define:0_atlast:;;at_last;nr01;0
0_define:0_mset:;;macroset;nr01;0
0_define:0_char:;;num2chr;nr01;0
0_define:0_program:;;program;nr01;0
END
IF:all
TEXT

Setting the defaults. These are different from the defaults of @code{m0}.
The second line defines the special characters for the arguments. This is missing the fifth character.
It is thus not possible to select arguments from other stacks than the first stack.

END
CODE
0_chars_pattern:{}-\@+*?~
0_chars_args:1%*@#;101;102;103;gpmexec
0_chars_args:1%*@#;101;102;103;gpmdef
0_chars_args:1$*@#$;111;112;103
END
HEADIF:Quoting in default macro set;all
TEXT

In the next part a macro with a pattern is used to have quoting in the macros in the
default macro set @code{0} operational.
The quoting is thus not a builtin function in @code{m0}. 

END
CODE
0_set_var:111;+-+
0_set_var:112;-+-
0_pattern:0string;{@00-@ff}~;stack_b 1 stack_a begin-1
0_pattern:0string;+-+;stack_b 1 +
0_pattern:0string;-+-;stack_b 1 - dup =0 if stack_a end stop endif
0_define:+-+;$1;;nn00 ;0string
END
HEADIF:Quoting;all
TEXT

In the next part a macro with a pattern is used to have quoting in @code{gpm} in
a similar way as @code{m6}.

END
CODE
0_set_var:101;<
0_set_var:102;>
0_set_var:103;:
0_pattern:string;{@00-@ff}~;stack_b 1 stack_a begin-1
0_pattern:string;<;stack_b 1 +
0_pattern:string;>;stack_b 1 - dup =0 if stack_a end stop endif
0_define:<;%1;;nn00 ;string;;;gpmexec
END
HEADIF:Collecting arguments of macros;all
TEXT

The collecting of arguments in @code{gpm} macros is executed with a pattern named @code{gpm} 
containing small programs.

This pattern will also recognise the arithmetic for the
@code{BAR} macro. It will push the operators to the
operator stack. The @code{BAR} macro can execute these
operators.


END
CODE
0_pattern:gpm;{@00-@ff}~;stack_a begin-1 
0_pattern:gpm;:;stack_a end begin  
0_pattern:gpm;];stack_a end stop
0_pattern:gpm;:+:;stack_a 0 oppush +
0_pattern:gpm;:-:;stack_a 0 oppush -
0_pattern:gpm;:*:;stack_a 0 oppush *
0_pattern:gpm;:/:;stack_a 0 oppush /
END
HEADIF:Substitution of arguments;all
TEXT

The emulation is not using the default argument substitution,
because the local defined macros should be deleted at the end
of a macro. The argument substitution is executed at the end
of a macro and is therefore the correct place to delete
local macros.

Stack @code{h} holds the names of macros defined. This stack is not
freed at the end of a @code{DEF}.

A char with value 0 is used in macros to execute the deletion of
the macros whereby the name of the macros are stored on stack @code{h}.

A char with value 3 is used in the @code{DEF} macro to execute the deletion of
macros and to store the defined macro on stack @code{h}.

A char with value 2 is used in the @code{VAL} macro to output
the second argument and delete the last char (the char with value 0).

END
CODE
0_define:0_zero;0_char:0
;;nn00;;;;0
0_define:0_three;0_char:3
;;nn00;;;;0
0_define:0_two;0_char:2
;;nn00;;;;0
0_pattern:arggpm;%{0-9};stack_b getlast1 getarg putout 
0_pattern:arggpm;0_zero;+-+ stack_a pop_to_2 "gpmdef"
stack_h while_st putarg1 "pop" fun_call pop endwhile
"" putout -+-
0_pattern:arggpm;0_three;+-+ stack_a pop_to_2 copyto_c "gpmdef"
stack_h while_st putarg1 "pop" fun_call pop endwhile
copyfr_c free_no "" putout -+-
0_pattern:arggpm;0_two;stack_b getarg2 putout "" putout
END
HEADIF:Main macro for executing all macros;all
TEXT

The main macro will collect all arguments including the macro name
and will make an @code{mcall} to the macro.

This is therefore a relatively simple macro.
END
CODE
0_def_mcall:[;;gpmdef;rr00;gpm;;gpmexec
END
HEADIF:DEF;all
TEXT

The macros will be defined in the @code{gpmdef} macro set.

They will be called from the main macro. Therefore the argument
collection has no significance.


A program to set all the options for the @code{define} or
@code{push} function is defined to be used by the @code{DEF} and
the @code{UPDATE} macro. The definition of a new macro is
appended with a zero character to activate the deletion of
macros at the end of a macro by the @code{arggpm} argument substitution.

END
CODE
0_program:defargs;+-+ 
stack_a pop_to_3 "" "nn00" "gpm" "arggpm" "" "gpmdef"
stack_c getarg2 -+-"0_zero"+-+ cat putarg2-+-
END
TEXT
The @code{DEF} uses the @code{push} function so that
it can also be popped at the end of macros.
END
CODE
0_define:DEF;0_three;push;nn00;gpm;arggpm; @defargs ;gpmdef
END
HEADIF:UPDATE;all
TEXT

Is similar to @code{DEF}, but uses a @code{define} instead of
a @code{push}.

END
CODE
0_define:UPDATE;0_zero;define;nn00;gpm;arggpm; @defargs ;gpmdef
END
HEADIF:BIN;all
TEXT

This macro has nothing to do. In @code{m0} integers and strings are 
automatically converted.

END
CODE
0_define:BIN;%10_zero;;nn00;gpm;arggpm;;gpmdef
END
HEADIF:DEC;all
TEXT

Similar to @code{BIN}.

END
CODE
0_define:DEC;%10_zero;;nn00;gpm;arggpm;;gpmdef
END
HEADIF:VAL;all
TEXT

Links to @code{info}, but needs to also output char with
0 to the output.

END
CODE
0_define:VAL;0_two0_zero;info;nn00;gpm;arggpm;recur_n;gpmdef
END
HEADIF:BAR;all
TEXT

The operators are already stored by the @code{gpm} pattern.
So operator needs only to be executed here.

END
CODE
0_define:BAR;%20_zero;;nn00;gpm;arggpm;stack_a opexall;gpmdef
END
HEADIF:end of emulation file, start of gpm files;all
TEXT

Finally switch to the @code{gpmexec} macro set.

END
CODE
0_mset:gpmexec
END
